    <section id="composite" class="panel">
      <h2>The value composite — what Screener.in doesn’t give you</h2>
      <p>Screener.in shows metrics one at a time; it can’t rank the whole market by a single blended cheapness score. That blended score is the point of this site.</p>
      <p>For each stock we convert every cheapness factor into a <b>yield</b> (so higher = cheaper), then take its <b>cross-sectional z-score</b> — how many standard deviations above/below the market average it sits. The composite is the <b>equal-weight mean of the z-scores the stock has</b>:</p>
      <pre class="formula">factors  = E/P, B/P, S/P, EBIT/EV, FCF-yield, 1/PEG   (div-yield display-only)

per factor:  yield = 1 / ratio        (E/P = 1/PE, etc.)
             z     = (yield − market mean) / market stdev

composite = mean( z[f]  for each factor f the stock HAS )
higher composite = cheaper overall</pre>
      <h3>Missing-value handling</h3>
      <p>A factor only counts when its ratio is valid and positive (PE, PB, PS, EV/EBIT &gt; 0). A stock missing a factor is averaged over the rest — never penalised with a zero. Small/micro caps have thinner data, so the <b>Data</b> column shows each row’s completeness.</p>
      <h3>Loss-maker exclusion</h3>
      <p>Negative or zero earnings and negative book don’t produce a valid yield, so <b>loss-makers never score “cheap”</b> on those factors — a stock isn’t cheap just because its price collapsed alongside its profits.</p>
      <h3>Custom weights (live)</h3>
      <p>The site ships the per-factor z-scores, so the sliders <b>recompute the composite in your browser</b> with no refetch. Default is equal weight; set FCF-yield to max and everything else to zero to rank purely on cash yield, for example.</p>
    </section>

